---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';
import { federationRows } from '../../lib/federation.js';

const page = codexPage('threshold-signatures');
const federations = federationRows(4, 10);
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    A federation has to sign as one: issue ecash notes, approve a withdrawal, agree on the chain. But no single guardian should be
    able to do that alone. A threshold signature solves both. The signing key is split into shares, one per guardian, and any
    <em>t</em> of the <em>n</em> shares together produce a signature that checks out against the federation’s single public key.
    Fedimint signs its ecash notes this way, with threshold blind signatures.<Cite page={page} s="fmTbs" />
  </p>

  <h2>How many signatures?</h2>
  <p>
    The threshold comes from how many faulty guardians the federation must survive. With <em>n</em> guardians it tolerates
    <em>f</em> = ⌊(<em>n</em> − 1) / 3⌋ of them, and it acts once <em>t</em> = <em>n</em> − <em>f</em> agree. Fedimint’s code
    defines exactly these two numbers.<Cite page={page} s="fmNumPeers" />
  </p>
  <p class="formula">t = n − ⌊(n − 1) / 3⌋</p>
  <p>That threshold does two jobs at once:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It is <strong>low enough</strong> that the honest guardians can always reach it, even if all <em>f</em> faulty ones stay silent.</li>
    <li>
      It is <strong>high enough</strong> that the faulty ones can never reach it on their own, and that any two groups of <em>t</em>
      signers share at least one honest guardian. That is the same arithmetic that makes Byzantine agreement possible when fewer
      than a third of the members are faulty.<Cite page={page} s="lsp" />
    </li>
  </ul>

  <figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Signatures needed for 4 to 10 guardians</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Guardians (n)</th><th scope="col">Faulty tolerated (f)</th><th scope="col">Signatures needed (t)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {federations.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.guardians}</th><td>{row.tolerated}</td><td>{row.threshold}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Seven guardians tolerate two faults and need five signatures; ten tolerate three and need seven.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How the game models it</h2>
  <p>
    On Signing Isle, withdrawals open while the match runs, each needing <em>t</em> signatures from the guardians still seated.
    Byzantine guardians file forged withdrawals that need the same number, so they must fool honest guardians into signing. An audit
    at the ledger desk tells you privately whether a withdrawal is real. The Academy’s Threshold Hall asks you to set the threshold
    for federations of 4 to 13 guardians until the formula feels obvious.
  </p>
</Article>
